Z||000000||Hippocrat||10-09-2001||10:42 PM||||My brothers 86 4cyl/5spd idles at 3000 Rpm NO matter what we do.<P>We have changed the following.<P>IAC<BR>MAP<BR>TPS<BR>ECM<BR>Even changed the throttle body and gasket. <BR>Changed the intake gasket (grasping I know)<BR>None of these fixed the issue. We have searched extensively for any vacuum leaks and found none. Even changed the EGR and gasket.<P>The throttle linkage is not touching the idler screw so drilling this out would not help.<P>There are two ways that we have managed to drop the idle to an almost tolerable level<P>1. Retard timing to a point where it idles but chokes when you actually try to drive it.<P>2. Short the ECM to diagnostic mode. <P>neither one is an acceptable (nor should be a needed) way to set the idle.<P>ANY? thoughts?<BR>Thanx<P>Oh we changed the distributor as well.<p>[This message has been edited by Hippocrat (edited 10-09-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000001||Mach10||10-09-2001||11:01 PM||||2 things:<P>1) Did you RESET the ECM? (disco battery for 10s)<BR>2) Check that your throttle cable isn't sticking. Ferrari_Cdn was doing something similar...<BR>3) Could be a Vacuum leak. Check all the hoses. Twice. Check the Vac. canister...<BR>4) Try changing O2 sensor... That's the only mixture sensing device you haven't changed. It gets turned off when you go to DIAG mode...<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 10-09-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000002||Joe Torma||10-09-2001||11:10 PM||||Sounds like the EGR to plenum tube.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hippocrat||10-09-2001||11:26 PM||||Sorry meant to mention that I've made sure the throttle cable was returning freely.<P>And yep reset the ECM after every change.<BR> <BR>I wasn't aware that the O2 sensor was cut out in diag mode <BR>Ill have to check that out ..<P>Can always check the EGR again as well<BR>Any other ideas .<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mach10||10-09-2001||11:37 PM||||Typically when the O2 goes, it starts outputting lean, and the ECM dumps so much fuel that it smokes black (on mine, anyway). I can see it EASILY going the other way, and reporting rich at idle, and having the ECM opening up the IAC... <P>The only other thing I can think of is a crack in the intake system behind the IAC... Hole in the PCV line? That'd be a leak...||||reg||6
Z||000005||Hippocrat||10-09-2001||11:56 PM||||Could be a hole in the PVC line I haven't checked that. But I have changed the intake and everything. <BR>Think my best bet may be the O2 sensor I know it has been changed recently but it was pulled from another motor not new. Could have been on its last legs. <P>The baffling thing is that the motor runs like a top no hesitation nothing from the exhaust. It is nice and smooth. Just running fast. <P>Think I'm going to drop some ridelin in the tank .. seems to work on hyperactive kids why not a hyperactive motor? ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mach10||10-10-2001||09:42 AM||||Ah, Methylphenidate... Haven't used THAT stuff in a while...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000007||Hippocrat||10-11-2001||10:32 PM||||Well its idled perfectly now<P>Got going through the schematics in the Pontiac repair manual and you'll never guess what.<P>On a long shot I checked the lead going to the temp sensor it was broke. <BR>(not a big surprise the temp gauge quit a while ago) this was not  a suspect because the temp gauge quit and the car was still running fine. Until of course he over heated the car. This is the reason we tore the engine down and rebuilt it. When we put it back in this is when it developed the idle problem. <P>This is also the main reason that our first target were vacuum leaks.<P>I (pure guess at this point) assume that the ECM was using some previous reading for the temp? and when power was disconnected to rebuild the motor it cleared the ECM.<P>So what do you guys think ? The ECM always thought the engine was cold, preventing it from coming off high idle. Still not sure why it was three grand though. <P>Any thoughts?||||reg||1
